{"path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment1.md","content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: shared `_lazyModules` still allows concurrent duplicate compiles — memoize the in-flight `Future`, not just the resolved module**

`_moduleFor()` (`wasm_shell.dart:370-391`) is check-then-await-then-assign:

```dart
final cached = _lazyModules[asset];
if (cached != null) return Ok(cached);
...
return Ok(_lazyModules[asset] = await loader(asset));
```

Now that this map is shared between the foreground shell and every job clone, two concurrent first-invocations of the same interpreter both observe `cached == null` and both run the loader — e.g. a background job compiling `python.wasm` while the user runs a foreground `python3`, or two sibling jobs started together. Both pay the full ~29 MB compile; the second write to the shared map wins and the loser's module is discarded after its run.

Within a single shell this window was previously unreachable (pipelines are sequential and `&` is a fail-fast parse error), so this PR newly makes double-compiles reachable — on iOS, the exact platform where #640 showed that JIT-compiling these modules exhausts the app VM limits. No correctness loss (compiled modules are immutable, instances are built per stage), but it transiently doubles compile CPU + memory and quietly defeats the PR's own "compile-once-per-app-run" goal in the job+foreground overlap case.

Suggested fix: cache the in-flight work instead of the result — store `Future<WasmModule>` per asset in the shared map, await it, and evict the entry on error so the "a failed lazy compile is not cached: the next invocation retries" contract still holds:

```dart
final existing = _lazyModules.putIfAbsent(asset, () => loader(asset));
try {
  return Ok(await existing);
} on Object {
  _lazyModules.remove(asset); // failed compile: retry next invocation
  rethrow;
}
```

(With a `Map<String, Future<WasmModule>>` type — the current call sites only ever read the resolved module.) A regression test would start a job whose loader blocks on a `Completer`, run a foreground `python3` concurrently, and assert the loader fired once.
